From: desilets@sj.ate.slb.com  (Mark Desilets)
Subject: Re:  are we really uniquely virtuous?

> 
> Mark Desilets:
> > Man is not unique in his urge to compete -- he is unique in two regards
> > 1) He is a highly effective competitor, able to develop nuclear weapons
> > to aid in his competition, if neccessary, and 2) He is the only species
> > that (as yet) has shown the capacity to restrain his competition.
> 
> 2a) Is there any other species whose "competition" risks wiping them out?
>
  No (on Earth), but I think I implied this in 1).  At least I meant to.  
  And I don't regard this as a Good Thing per se, just an inevitable one.
  My original point was that evolution was competitive, man is the local
  winner in this competition (from a "power" standpoint), and that a 
  competitive species is likely to produce individuals who act in their
  own self interest.  To me, this belief lends credence to the view that
  if there were other species in our little corner of the universe, and
  they were markedly more advanced than are we, we would know about it.
  
> 2b) Many animals -- dogs, for instance -- have strategies for avoiding death
> in their battles for rank.

  Point taken, but these strategies are more in the line of unconditional
  surrender (going belly-up) than in the line of negotiated settlement.


							Regards,

								Mark

