Date: Mon, 11 Jan 93 10:41:25 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: I&A: Turning Ideas into Reality

Ideas and Applications: Turning Ideas into Reality

or, "Why don't Extropians ever _do_ anything?"

or, to paraphrase the German original, "What have you done for the
future today?"


Why don't we ever _do_ anything? Why do we just talk and talk? Where
have I heard _this_ before!

First of all, I really resent this kind of remark! Just last week I
put the finishing touches on a diamondoid-fullerine nanoscale general
assembler...I'll release it out into the world as soon as the bugs in
the self-replicating part are worked out. And I began construction of
a Dyson sphere a couple of months ago. Oh, and I'm writing this in
virtual reality from my ocean colonization platform achored off the
coast of scenic Aptos, California (known to the mundanes as the
"cement ship," the "Palo Alto"...really...check it out!). As soon as I
finish uploading myself into my Mac IIci, I'll start work on some of
the _really_ tough problems!

So don't tell me Extropians never implement their ideas!

Seriously, there are some important points to be made:

1. A disproportionately large fraction of folks on this list _are_, in
fact, doing interesting things. Many are computer types (no surprise),
some are practicing professors, others are scientists, several have
written books (off the top of my head: Moravec, Friedman, Raymond,
Cox), and many others seem to have active, rewarding careers.  To
dismiss them because they haven't worked on Dyson spheres, or uploaded
themselves, or whatever, seems overly harsh.

Ironically, many of the frequent posters have also led influential
lives, judging by these same criteria, so it's not just that the
successful people are too busy to post!

(I'm sure the folks who ask "What have Extropians really _done_?" are
not being either malicious or stupid...just raising this as a
rhetorical device to engage in debate. That's the spirit in which I'm
responding now.)

2. Many of the ideas discussed in this group are inherently long-range
and difficult to implement. We could of course change the focus of the
llist to very short-range, more immediately realizable ideas, but who
would remain to read the list? By this I mean that short-range stuff
tends to be either political (getting someone elected), or
business-oriented (how can Dell Computer increase market share in the
486DX-50 market niche), or of interest to only a very small percentage
of the list (make up your examples of a short term interest you have
and ask how many others on the list share your interest). Short-range
topics are already well-covered in many, many newsgroups, ranging from
misc.invest to talk.politics.*** to comp.lsi. 

3. I favor the stuff that will come about, perhaps, in my lifetime,
i.e., the next 40 years. (Yes, cryonics & nanotech _may_ increase
this, but the odds are not very good on this...another debate.)

This is why I generally stay out of the threads involving uploading
(not gonna happen anytime soon!), Dyson spheres (ultra ditto), and
similar far-off ideas. I completely tune out when the perennial
debates about making millions of copies of one's self flare up. But to
each their own...that's the beauty of free markets.

But I also favor discussing things that are more than just a year or
two away--mostly for the reasons just cited above. Things that are
only a few years off tend to be highly specific, to depend on a lot of
detail that list members here can't have or be expected to learn, and
are of limited interest.

4. Some discussions of products (like the Hip PC--made by a list
member, Doug Platt, which provides more evidence that the list has
doers as well as talkers) and personal fitness/health issues
(vitamins, exercise, drugs, etc.) obviously have short-term horizons.
(And it seems a fair chunk of the traffic on this list is about just
these short-term "tips" being shared.)

5. The issue of "How can we make _money_ on these ideas?" comes up
often, with various proposals that we on this list (somehow) get
together, pool our ideas, pool our money (no thanks...), and (somehow)
_fund_ an Extropian startup company! Needless to say, there are always
going to be people with ideas they think should be funded by
others...sort of like the folks who approach authors with their ideas
that "just need to be fleshed out."

Well, several friends of mine are in the midst of getting companies
going, and it's not something to be done casually, or to be planned in
public in a relatively low-bandwidth environment like this list (a
point Perry Metzger made several weeks ago). Getting even the simplest
ideas developed to the point of attracting funding is a huge chore.
Getting a really "Extropian" idea funded, something like ocean-going mining
vessels, is almost an impossible idea.

(Yes, advanced ideas _do_ get funded. But not many. And generally in
such a way that intermediate, profit-making steps are clearly
apparent. Nobody funded Boeing in 1935, or whenever, with the "goal"
of producing the 747...it was funded to make money within a couple of
years, several at the most. Ditto for Intel, Apple, Amgen, etc. You've
seen, perhaps, my essays on this list about the "desert" that may
exist between between "now" and "then" in some product development
spaces, so I won't repeat the arguments here.)

6. Consensus on what's important to fund (and note that "important" is
not necessarily the same as "profitable") is lacking on this list. As
expected, as we all have different ideas. There are small clusters of
folks who support forming new drug companies to make smart drugs,
others who want to launch ocean colonies, VR supporters, and so on. It
is unlikely a bunch of us will hit upon a reasonable idea and be able
to fund it, for all the reasons already mentioned (and some more).

7. We cannot reasonably expect casually introduced ideas to somehow
energize a large fraction of the list to form a company.

8. Many on this list _are_ pushing the envelope in the best way
possible, for them, by working in labs, writing books, developing
products, and even by writing pieces for this list.


Well, having said all this, let me conclude by saying that I, too,
would welcome the chance to get involved with some truly revolutionary
startup company. I had 12 stimulating and enjoyable years at Intel,
during the incredible excitement of the invention of the
microprocessor. I see the stuff in VR and cyberspace as offering the
greatest potential--but also the greatest hype. The recent problems at
VPL Research (shut down and absorbed into Thompson-CSF, the French
company), Xanadu (still no product, after 5 years, and no money), AMIX
(a product, but not enough customers, and with funds due to run out
completely right around _now_), and elsewhere should make us all a bit
wary about "neat ideas" making real money.

I have some money to invest. Most of it's in stocks. If I, a putative
futurist and member of this list, see nothing obvious (so far)
to invest in, of the Extropian ideas, that is, then why should
outsiders?

These comments are not at all meant to be negative, or dismissive. In
fact, this list is doing about the best I can imagine, despite the
obvious S/N problems that crop up. Ideas are easy to generate, real
businesses are hard to build. Especially in new areas.

Those of you who float what you think is a good idea, like "aptical
foddering," and then wonder why the list doesn't embrace your idea and
rush to fund it, should not get discouraged. Maybe aptical foddering
is just an idea whose time has not yet come. And a lot of work is
needed before looking for actual investment.

So don't give up.

Cheers,

-Tim

