Dani Eder wondered what would happen if guns were distributed to
people living in a dictatorship.  This very idea was attempted
toward the end of WWII.

The US government had General Motors produce tens-of-thousands of
cheap, one-shot .45s called "Liberators" (I like that name).
Except for the barrel and springs, all the parts were stamped out
of sheet metal.  Total cost?  Under two bucks.  For those living
in, or visiting, the San Francisco Bay Area, a Liberator is on
display at the "San Francisco Gun Exchange" a gun store in
downtown SF.  (They also have many other historical an unusual
guns in their collection.  My two favorites are the Liberator and
the Gyrojet.  The latter was a pistol that shot rockets instead
of bullets.  Unfortunately it never made it commercially.)

The plan was to air-drop Liberators into captive countries.  They
came with pictorial directions to instruct the local resistance
fighters on their use.  In cartoon form, the instructions showed
a resistance fighter blowing away an enemy soldier and then
capturing his REAL gun to use in more serious social situations.

The war ended before any substantial distributions could be done.
It is obvious, however, that the idea held enough merit in the
opinion of the US military, to justify the program.

